Agent: This is a meaningful project — there are several strong routes you can take to research a wartime refugee’s story. Below I’ve grouped practical steps you can follow, archival resources to check, and tips for making requests. If you want I can draft request letters or search queries once you have a few key facts (see “What to gather” below).

1) Gather the essentials first (what to collect)
- Full name (including maiden/alternate spellings), approximate birth date/place, parents’ names if known  
- Any immigration details you already have: year of arrival, port of entry (New York, Baltimore, etc.), ship name (if known), first U.S. address, naturalization date/place, Social Security number, any documents (naturalization certificate, passport, refugee papers, obituary)  
- Family stories, letters, photographs, or the name of the relative who helped sponsor or receive her — these clues are often crucial.

2) U.S. records to check (high yield)
- Passenger arrival / ship manifests (Ellis Island, Castle Garden, NARA passenger lists). Many manifests are indexed on Ancestry.com and FamilySearch.org.  
- Naturalization records — NARA and local county courts; USCIS Genealogy Program can search N–400/N–600 files and issue copies of older files.  
- Social Security application (Form SS‑5) — can provide birth name, birthplace, parents’ names (request via the Social Security Administration).  
- Federal & state census records, city directories, local newspapers, and death/obituary notices (use local library archives, Newspapers.com, GenealogyBank, or Chronicling America).  
- Local courts (where she lived) for any naturalization or legal filings.

3) International / German sources
- Arolsen Archives (formerly International Tracing Service, IT S/Bad Arolsen) — holds displaced persons (DP) and refugee records from WWII and after; has an increasingly searchable digital service and can process inquiries. Very important for refugees and DP camp records.  
- German civil registry (Standesamt) or church (Kirchenbuch) records for births/marriage — contact the appropriate municipality where she was born. Note German privacy/archival access rules (some recent records are restricted).  
- Bundesarchiv and regional state archives — for records of expulsions/evacuations, refugee movements in the postwar period (depends on region).  
- JewishGen and regional genealogical societies (if applicable) — excellent for Jewish refugee traces.

4) Holocaust / refugee specialized archives & organizations
- United States Holocaust Memorial Museum (USHMM) — archives, survivor databases, displacement records and help with searches.  
- Red Cross tracing / American Red Cross archives — sometimes helped reunite refugees and kept records.  
- International Organization for Migration (IOM) historical programs or the UN Refugee archives for postwar resettlement programs.

5) Practical search & request tips
- Search variant spellings, Anglicized names, and name order differences (e.g., surname first).  
- Use a timeframe window (e.g., arrivals 1945–1955) when searching manifests or DP lists.  
- When contacting archives: include full name, approximate dates/places, relationship to you, copies of any documents you already have, and a clear, polite request. Note processing times can be weeks–months and may involve small fees.  
- For Germany requests: ask the local Standesamt (civil registry) where she was born/registered. If you don’t know the exact town, Arolsen and regional archives may help narrow it down.

6) How to organize and proceed
- Start with what you can access quickly: family members for oral history, any documents you already own, U.S. passenger lists on FamilySearch/Ancestry, and a Social Security (SS‑5) request.  
- Simultaneously submit a search request to Arolsen Archives if you suspect DP/resettlement involvement — they often hold the most direct refugee records.  
- If you find a likely ship manifest or naturalization, follow the lead to county court records for the full file.

7) DNA and community approaches
- If you’ve done DNA (23andMe/AncestryDNA), use matches and messaging to connect with distant relatives who may hold family lore or documents.  
- Local historical societies or refugee/migrant community groups (in the U.S. city she settled) can sometimes provide leads (sponsorship groups, refugee resettlement agencies).

8) Things to expect
- Not every thread leads to a complete story; wartime and postwar records can be fragmented.  
- Privacy laws mean some records (recent civil records) may be restricted; older records are easier to access.  
- Some searching is iterative — one record often leads to the next.

9) If you’d like, I can help with next steps
- Draft a request for USCIS Genealogy Program or Arolsen Archives.  
- Compose search queries for Ancestry/FamilySearch passenger lists or NARA indexes.  
- Create an interview/question sheet to use when talking to relatives to capture useful clues.

If you want to proceed, tell me which of these you’d like help with first (example: “Help me search passenger lists” or “Draft an Arolsen inquiry”) and share any details you already have about your great-aunt (name, birth year/place, approximate year she arrived in the U.S.). I’ll help you craft the next action.
